#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064003 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "48ec6930-88b7-5aad-9bb7-c5e23e766f80" :question/math-academy-id "e-470" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $9x^2 = (3x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\,\\textrm d u=\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(3x)^{2}}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡3x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/id #uuid "3e89a278-54ab-59a6-adb6-38f43c0fbe9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-471" :question/problem "Calculate the integral  $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{3}{2(1 + 25x^2)} = \\dfrac 3 2 \\int \\dfrac{1}{1+25x^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^2}\\textrm{d}u =\\arctan u +C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $25x^2 = (5x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 5\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{2+50{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡5x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064005 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "cb771ff3-b5a9-5900-913f-df23957760b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-472" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt 2} \\dfrac{1}{2+x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+{x}^{2}/2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this integral now resembles the basic integral for the inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{1+u^2}\\,\\textrm d u = \\arctan{u} + C.\n$$\n\nWe make the substitution $u=\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt 2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\sqrt 2 \\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064014 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/id #uuid "56860611-869d-5035-bf8f-f568c774d78d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5014" :question/problem "${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{5\\, \\textrm{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^2-1}}  = }$" :question/worked-solution "This resembles the basic integral for $\\textrm{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nLet $u = 5x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n5\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5\\,\\text{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}} & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db06961f-0dd2-5efc-be06-4e0611964fe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287465} {:answer/id #uuid "5c97499b-3e30-5cef-a80a-c25520c3e23f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287466} {:answer/id #uuid "cf93e1a6-1bbf-5a81-8c7b-5fe67a0988c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287467} {:answer/id #uuid "352bf025-f82e-50ad-8c77-25bd6188beb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287468} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7fbc93-a0d1-54a6-a36d-45226c125827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db06961f-0dd2-5efc-be06-4e0611964fe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287465} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b9fefbf-0fd1-58ea-a9e5-d4f208246cd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71e110b8-dac7-55ea-9c66-c30aaca2c79e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114023" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1-36{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $36{x}^{2}=(6x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=6x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1-36{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(6x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38b972ee-a867-51b2-a219-e9ccfd78ce49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287472} {:answer/id #uuid "99453bfd-a25b-5bc8-994d-7eed3cd45a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287473} {:answer/id #uuid "3c7503b1-07cc-54df-9d22-28cf5115da96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287474} {:answer/id #uuid "5487628f-5c30-5a0d-be78-3425665e5133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287475} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0ef784-2458-5a50-a83d-d0303278b839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38b972ee-a867-51b2-a219-e9ccfd78ce49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287472} :answer-field/id #uuid "705c21dc-4764-585e-a0a3-ed10ace4c168" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bbe4eea-ad7c-5a02-9125-5359b7a00ff4" :question/math-academy-id "q-114024" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $2{x}^{2}=(\\sqrt{2}x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{2}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(\\sqrt{2}x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d903e01-4898-5f10-ab2c-5b2d86d25edc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287479} {:answer/id #uuid "7b4b64b8-6208-5962-87dc-868d2aeba449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(9x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287480} {:answer/id #uuid "20e248db-fe8a-5f4e-8517-5f6250caab76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{81}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(9x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287481} {:answer/id #uuid "470bdc7e-c8c5-51b5-9d5b-a129fc52fe2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(9x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287482} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9e27ef-a03f-57ee-8cf5-c12adcf28b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(9x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20e248db-fe8a-5f4e-8517-5f6250caab76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{81}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(9x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287481} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c09f0e5-daf4-524b-bed6-3fed93c072eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23b873d8-ac0f-5aee-8252-f929246de6fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-114025" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{9\\sqrt{1-81{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $81{x}^{2}=(9x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=9x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{9\\sqrt{1-81{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(9x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(9x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbfbca95-5141-5253-a86e-9584a7b510a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287486} {:answer/id #uuid "26b0ffcc-656d-5568-abda-f8a626d1b9f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287487} {:answer/id #uuid "86099f70-f073-5f4e-b798-8a5e09c08614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(3x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287488} {:answer/id #uuid "69dd2123-1bcd-5dc9-81fc-a2533bf9265d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(3x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287489} {:answer/id #uuid "187e2440-fa88-5df3-9582-c6cab739bf44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "187e2440-fa88-5df3-9582-c6cab739bf44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287490} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c7fdf3-5881-51bf-a348-fdc8cc1084f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34369d01-da71-5054-b6e7-8a4b327608db" :question/math-academy-id "q-114027" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{1-3{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $3{x}^{2}=(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{3}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{1-3{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{1-(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{3}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61400a3-b4de-54ea-b92a-2aaddcd20daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(9x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287493} {:answer/id #uuid "28a8ac60-0116-5e76-8bd0-8240616a4ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287494} {:answer/id #uuid "742eb3c8-dc80-5fa9-ad36-9329b3c7002e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287495} {:answer/id #uuid "ed595cf4-8bc0-52f4-ad0d-ea6680beeb00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287496} {:answer/id #uuid "f60a0147-29d7-5482-8679-51b7608e7d2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f60a0147-29d7-5482-8679-51b7608e7d2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287497} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c6f62f-0ddf-58c2-8ebd-7d6080a86152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "773c49c1-4feb-58a3-abe3-1d48220dd1dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-114046" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{9}{81+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{81+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{9}{81\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/81)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/81}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/9)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\n The only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{9})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{9}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{9}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=9\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{81+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/9)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (9\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29ee8153-af01-53fa-afe5-b4270151bafa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287500} {:answer/id #uuid "76658b0f-bdb1-5178-a04c-e74d20b5614b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{5}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287501} {:answer/id #uuid "5d032742-72a0-5631-a227-587bdd5dffd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287502} {:answer/id #uuid "75156ac2-1acc-5fc2-81d1-afc274fa4cc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{5}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287503} {:answer/id #uuid "556bc950-a93a-5a12-a14d-a56c0183c7a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{5}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76658b0f-bdb1-5178-a04c-e74d20b5614b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{5}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287501} :answer-field/id #uuid "2390826e-c3f6-5f7e-975a-d4d0aba4a2f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "604712d6-42d9-5fa2-83bd-a9f37f01b70e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114051" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|x|\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-1}}=\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{|\\sqrt{5}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{5}x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\sqrt{5}x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{5}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{|\\sqrt{5}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{5}x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{5}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc1000c5-88fa-5b61-b927-4f9b6af58222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{2}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287507} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5b1a4e-ce90-529a-b35c-5f36831a398a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287508} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c7c4cc-ac17-5be1-a0db-21066560bb06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{2}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287509} {:answer/id #uuid "44817563-f828-5d59-aac7-7415b51b8651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{arcsec}(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287510} {:answer/id #uuid "ef192795-59a8-57fa-82bc-82933c3acf28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{2}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef192795-59a8-57fa-82bc-82933c3acf28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{2}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287511} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b35ff24-bfab-5af9-8679-5d4f9a36220c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a74e58b3-5ef7-5ad8-b1f8-d9f6bfa9ce10" :question/math-academy-id "q-114052" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|x|\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-1}}=\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{|\\sqrt{2}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2}x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\sqrt{2}x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{2}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{|\\sqrt{2}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2}x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{2}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef617db5-0efb-5b3e-891e-f4998d44f325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287514} {:answer/id #uuid "954de688-318e-5150-8224-414ec4b16932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287515} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9b0fb9-7d8c-548e-8b01-c63a6da40345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287516} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f04de8-eac3-56f3-bf93-acb11e4eecd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287517} {:answer/id #uuid "c38d5173-e586-577f-9b0e-511dd492f8ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "954de688-318e-5150-8224-414ec4b16932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287515} :answer-field/id #uuid "41ba77f5-09ca-52c2-aced-ebbb5ccc0204" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8a2299c-2891-5f11-90dc-7964d7987169" :question/math-academy-id "q-19393" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/16)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/16}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-5\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be85f47e-071e-570f-92cb-fbee2f0947fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061410} {:answer/id #uuid "c9c5ec91-6dae-5c68-aabf-2369c2f4cf95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061411} {:answer/id #uuid "d21e4bc6-9adb-57fd-aed2-333af51b67fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061412} {:answer/id #uuid "73721344-1d4a-53c7-849c-c4c979de6860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061413} {:answer/id #uuid "786466cd-1f50-5bf1-b009-4fcc531fe655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9c5ec91-6dae-5c68-aabf-2369c2f4cf95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061411} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8187d0f-e969-5a26-98c6-b8cd07a6a116" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aa336cf9-b3b8-5d4a-a543-f9cee172da34" :question/math-academy-id "q-19394" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28755659-c143-5759-b8da-7a530f51178a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287521} {:answer/id #uuid "95d98d3d-fa42-53cc-b91b-f5819bbbe3ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287522} {:answer/id #uuid "078315a8-7668-5374-8553-215ae24f1dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287523} {:answer/id #uuid "82d9a02a-225b-58df-9f5a-2b34f14a5198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287524} {:answer/id #uuid "88c31943-4f4f-5a51-bd33-5fa2048fc973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88c31943-4f4f-5a51-bd33-5fa2048fc973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287525} :answer-field/id #uuid "4150c91b-cc6e-56f7-958b-25e87b0b3a8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b84d22c-bb4f-5513-8652-92de66383ce5" :question/math-academy-id "q-19396" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\frac{20}{3+75{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can write this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\frac{20}{3+75{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{20}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $25{x}^{2}=(5x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\frac{20}{3+75{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{20}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\frac{1}{1+(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80ae4e27-fd44-59c4-95a3-19ea734ef266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\arctan (14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061416} {:answer/id #uuid "b34b69ba-1ef6-5818-baa4-336cac499183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\arctan (7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061417} {:answer/id #uuid "04964092-53d7-5fd9-b2a5-0efd74895ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\arctan (14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061418} {:answer/id #uuid "0d681a7a-e503-5ba0-84a2-cee035a46c0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\arctan (7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061419} {:answer/id #uuid "4c79f4e2-40e2-5dfe-bf69-7a5344a00a0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\arctan (14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80ae4e27-fd44-59c4-95a3-19ea734ef266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\arctan (14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061416} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f3bce0d-ec40-5c5e-90b3-936911cc2ce7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4167c2cd-a204-5a1b-9ee4-1fca54336ae6" :question/math-academy-id "q-19397" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{9}{2+8{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{9}{2+8{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $4{x}^{2}=(2x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $14$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{9}{2+8{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{14}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{14}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{14} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(14)-\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(14)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "917cb601-d5eb-572d-bf4f-bb06a638fc75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287528} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e2fd85-f260-5896-b563-68fad839d972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287529} {:answer/id #uuid 